				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h2 style="text-align: center;">How to Pick the Right One</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Room size:</strong> Small spaces suit window or portable units; bigger rooms or whole homes call for split, cassette, or central systems.</li>
<li><strong>Budget:</strong> Portable and window units cost less upfront; inverter and central systems save more over the long run.</li>
<li><strong>Weather:</strong> For hot, humid climates like North Texas, inverter models perform well; mixed climates may suit a hybrid system.</li>
<li><strong>Energy use:</strong> Check the SEER rating — higher numbers mean less electricity used per ton of cooling.</li>
<li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> Split and cassette units need periodic cleaning; portable units are easiest to move and maintain.</li>
<li><strong>Smart features:</strong> If remote control and scheduling matter to you, a WiFi-enabled smart thermostat is worth the small upgrade.</li>
</ul></div>
